import{_ as t,o as a,c as s,O as i}from"./chunks/framework.4XV3vnnU.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Concepts","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"documentation/concepts.md","filePath":"documentation/concepts.md","lastUpdated":1793117788000}'),n={name:"documentation/concepts.md"};function o(r,e,d,l,h,p){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="concepts" tabindex="-1">Concepts <a class="header-anchor" href="#concepts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Concepts&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Naira is a catalog of AI assets and the relationships between them. This page explains the vocabulary used throughout the portal, the API and the plugin code.</p><h2 id="the-catalog" tabindex="-1">The catalog <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-catalog" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The catalog&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalog is a graph. Its vertices are <strong>nodes</strong>, its edges are <strong>relations</strong>. The catalog service owns the graph and exposes it through the <a href="/pr-preview/pr-12/documentation/api">Catalog API</a>. It never talks to external systems itself. Everything in the graph was reported by a <a href="#plugins">plugin</a>.</p><h2 id="nodes" tabindex="-1">Nodes <a class="header-anchor" href="#nodes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Nodes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A node represents one asset: a model, a dataset, an inference endpoint, an MCP server, a Kubernetes deployment, a Git repository and so on.</p><p>A node is identified by its <strong>kind</strong> and its <strong>path</strong>:</p><ul><li>The <strong>kind</strong> says what sort of asset it is, for example <code>model</code> or <code>inference_endpoint</code>. Kinds are plain strings and must not contain a slash.</li><li>The <strong>path</strong> identifies the asset within its kind. It may contain slashes and typically starts with a prefix that identifies the source, for example <code>litellm/gpt-4o</code> or <code>mlflow/fraud-detector</code>.</li></ul><p>Together they form the resource name used by the API, <code>nodes/&lt;kind&gt;/&lt;path&gt;</code>. Identity is stable: the same asset reported in another run, or by another plugin, maps to the same node.</p><p>The kinds in use today are defined in the plugin API package and shared by all plugins:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Represents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>A model, as known by a registry, gateway or serving system</td></tr><tr><td><code>inference_endpoint</code></td><td>A place where a model can be called, together with its usage and health</td></tr><tr><td><code>dataset</code></td><td>A dataset from a data catalog or experiment tracker</td></tr><tr><td><code>mcp_server</code>, <code>mcp_tool</code></td><td>An MCP server and the tools it exposes</td></tr><tr><td><code>deployment</code>, <code>service</code></td><td>Kubernetes workloads and the services in front of them</td></tr><tr><td><code>application</code></td><td>An application that groups workloads</td></tr><tr><td><code>git_repository</code></td><td>A source repository on GitHub or Hugging Face</td></tr><tr><td><code>Kustomization.fluxcd</code>, <code>HelmChart.fluxcd</code>, <code>GitRepository.fluxcd</code></td><td>FluxCD objects</td></tr><tr><td><code>owner</code></td><td>A team or person owning other nodes</td></tr><tr><td><code>tech_radar</code>, <code>tech_radar_entry</code></td><td>The Tech Radar and its entries</td></tr></tbody></table><p>New kinds can be introduced by plugins. The portal groups kinds into <a href="/pr-preview/pr-12/documentation/portal#catalogs">catalogs</a>.</p><h2 id="relations" tabindex="-1">Relations <a class="header-anchor" href="#relations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Relations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A relation is a directed, typed edge between two nodes. It is identified by its kind, its source node and its target node. Relation kinds describe how assets depend on each other:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>serves_model</code></td><td>An inference endpoint serves a model</td></tr><tr><td><code>uses_model</code></td><td>A deployment calls a model</td></tr><tr><td><code>trained_on</code></td><td>A model was trained on a dataset</td></tr><tr><td><code>exposes</code></td><td>An MCP server exposes a tool</td></tr><tr><td><code>calls</code></td><td>A deployment calls a service</td></tr><tr><td><code>deployed_from</code>, <code>built_from</code>, <code>sourced_from</code></td><td>A workload or model comes from a FluxCD object, a repository or a Hugging Face repo</td></tr><tr><td><code>references</code>, <code>describes</code>, <code>derived_from</code>, <code>owned_by</code></td><td>Further links between repositories, documentation, lineage and owners</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both ends of a relation must exist in the catalog. Plugins therefore report the nodes a relation points at in the same run.</p><h2 id="properties-and-plugin-claims" tabindex="-1">Properties and plugin claims <a class="header-anchor" href="#properties-and-plugin-claims" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Properties and plugin claims&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Nodes and relations carry <strong>properties</strong>, a flat set of string key-value pairs such as a model&#39;s provider, a deployment&#39;s namespace or an endpoint&#39;s token usage.</p><p>Several plugins can report the same node. A LiteLLM plugin and an MLflow plugin may both know a model, each with different details. The catalog does not pick a winner. It keeps every plugin&#39;s properties separately, as a <strong>plugin claim</strong>, and returns them side by side:</p><div class="language-json vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">json</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">{</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;name&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;nodes/model/litellm/claude-sonnet&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;kind&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;model&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;path&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;litellm/claude-sonnet&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">,</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">  &quot;pluginClaims&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: [</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;plugin&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;litellm&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;props&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;provider&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;anthropic&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;mode&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;chat&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> } },</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;plugin&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;depl-uses-litellm&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;props&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">&quot;verified&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;true&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> } }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  ]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The portal shows claims per plugin on the detail page, so it is always clear where a piece of information came from.</p><h2 id="plugins" tabindex="-1">Plugins <a class="header-anchor" href="#plugins" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plugins&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A plugin connects one external system to the catalog. It runs as a sidecar container next to the catalog, reads from its system (an HTTP API, the Kubernetes API, a cloud provider or a file) and returns what it found. The catalog calls each plugin over gRPC with a single operation, <code>Collect</code>, and stores the result.</p><p>Plugins run on a <strong>schedule</strong> expressed as a cron expression, or on demand from the portal and the API. Each run is tracked as an <strong>operation</strong> with a start time, an end time, a result or an error. The Plugins page shows the latest operation per plugin.</p><p>Plugins are configured through environment variables. Most of them take a <code>PATH_PREFIX</code> so that several instances of the same plugin, for example two LiteLLM gateways, produce distinct node paths. See <a href="/pr-preview/pr-12/documentation/plugins">Plugins</a> for the list and <a href="/pr-preview/pr-12/documentation/plugin-authoring">Writing a Plugin</a> for the contract.</p><h2 id="snapshots" tabindex="-1">Snapshots <a class="header-anchor" href="#snapshots" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Snapshots&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every plugin run is an atomic snapshot of what the plugin currently sees. The catalog replaces that plugin&#39;s previous contribution with the new one: nodes and relations the plugin no longer reports are removed, claims of other plugins on the same nodes stay untouched. A failed run changes nothing, so the last good snapshot stays visible until the next successful run.</p><h2 id="catalogs-and-viewpoints" tabindex="-1">Catalogs and viewpoints <a class="header-anchor" href="#catalogs-and-viewpoints" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Catalogs and viewpoints&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A <strong>catalog</strong> in the portal is a viewpoint on the graph: a selection of kinds and of the plugins that feed them. The Model Catalog shows <code>model</code> nodes from the LiteLLM, MLflow and Bedrock plugins, the MCP Catalog shows <code>mcp_server</code> nodes and their tools, and so on. Viewpoints are configuration in the UI, not separate data stores. The same node can appear in more than one viewpoint.</p><h2 id="tech-radar" tabindex="-1">Tech Radar <a class="header-anchor" href="#tech-radar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tech Radar&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Tech Radar is special in that its source is not an external system but a reviewed YAML file. Its plugin turns the file into <code>tech_radar</code> and <code>tech_radar_entry</code> nodes, and entries can reference the catalog nodes they talk about. See <a href="/pr-preview/pr-12/documentation/tech-radar">Tech Radar</a>.</p><h2 id="storage" tabindex="-1">Storage <a class="header-anchor" href="#storage" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Storage&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalog keeps its graph in memory. After a restart it is empty until the plugins have run again, either on schedule or triggered by hand. The Helm chart ships a PostgreSQL database and the catalog is being prepared to persist its graph there.</p>`,33)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",o]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
